idea:
Combine the two precision trims that each came out slightly below the best (experiments 4 and 6) into one consensus filter, and replace implicit cycling with an explicit fill to T. Leave the classifier, gate, shuffle detection, spam/non-ASCII checks and dedup unchanged.

A. After `keep` is computed and before dedup, tokenize with tiktoken gpt2 `encode_batch` (`disallowed_special=()`): all target_sample docs, and every kept pool doc capped at 24,000 chars (first 8k + middle 8k + last 8k). For each doc compute:
(1) chars per token;
(2) unigram-token bits per byte, using add-0.5 smoothed counts from target tokens plus kept-pool tokens down-weighted to equal mass, with target docs scored leave-one-out;
(3) OOV rate of lowercase alphabetic words against V = words in the target sample or in at least 3 kept docs;
(4) uppercase-letter fraction;
(5) digit-char fraction;
(6) fraction of single-character non-punctuation tokens.
For each statistic, take the target median m and s = 1.4826*MAD, and set the allowed range to [min(p1, m - 5s), max(p99, m + 5s)]. Compute z = the largest normalised exceedance over the six statistics. Drop a kept doc if any statistic is outside its range.
Safety: if this would drop more than 15% of kept tokens, drop only the worst 15% by z. Skip the whole step if elapsed time exceeds 125 s.

B. Use exact token counts (scaled by full length / tokenized length) for the kept docs.

C. Explicit fill when kept tokens K < T: output floor(T/K) full passes over the kept set, each with a different seeded shuffle. Fill the remaining fraction with the kept docs that have the highest `score - z` (the cleanest, most target-like), shuffled, and place this partial pass FIRST so the full passes come last. If K >= T, behave as now.

Log to stderr: kept docs and K/T before and after the trim, number dropped per statistic, and number of passes.

Make the repeat passes purity-weighted instead of uniform. Leave the classifier, gate, shuffle detection, 5s consensus trim, dedup and the K >= T branch unchanged. Only the fill when K < T changes.

(1) In `_trim_stats`, also return a second exceedance matrix computed against a tight range per statistic: [max(lo, min(p5, m - 2.5s)), min(hi, max(p95, m + 2.5s))], using target percentiles p5/p95 and the same m and s. Let z_tight be the largest tight exceedance per doc.

(2) Define the core set as the kept (post-dedup) docs that satisfy all of:
- z_tight == 0, i.e. inside the tight range on all six statistics;
- title matches `_TITLE`;
- rate1 >= the 10th percentile of the leave-one-out clean target bigram rate (`clean_r`);
- dec >= the median dec of the kept docs minus 1.0.
If the core holds less than 40% of the kept tokens, relax it to the top 40% of kept tokens ranked by (score - z_tight). If the trim was skipped, core = top 60% by score.

(3) Fill: output exactly ONE full pass over all kept docs (seeded shuffle) FIRST, so every clean doc is seen once. Then fill the remaining T - K tokens only with core docs: floor((T-K)/Kcore) full core passes, each with its own seeded shuffle, plus a final partial core pass made of the highest (score - z_tight) core docs, shuffled, with the 0.5% T margin. The core passes come last, so the low-learning-rate end of training is on the cleanest, most target-typical text.

Log to stderr: core doc count, Kcore/T, K/T, the number of core passes, and how many docs fail each of the four core conditions.
